<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <RootNamespace>Vixen.AotProbe</RootNamespace>
        <AssemblyName>vixen-aot-probe</AssemblyName>
        <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
        <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
        <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>

        <!--
            Declared here rather than passed as `-p:PublishAot=true`, and that is not a style
            preference. A property on the command line is a *global* property: MSBuild hands it to
            every project in the graph, including the netstandard2.1 source generators, and a
            generator asked to compile ahead of time fails with NETSDK1207. Declaring it in the one
            project that means it keeps it where it belongs.
        -->
        <PublishAot>true</PublishAot>
        <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>

        <!--
            The whole point. ILC reports IL2xxx/IL3xxx as warnings by default and a warning nobody
            fails on is a warning nobody reads, which is the state doc 00 calls out by name.
        -->
        <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
        <ILLinkTreatWarningsAsErrors>true</ILLinkTreatWarningsAsErrors>
        <TrimmerSingleWarn>false</TrimmerSingleWarn>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!--
        The runtime-profile assemblies rooted today — 82 of the 95, measured 2026-09-10 (#506). It
        was 29, and the sixty-six that were missing were not a reasoned exclusion list; they were
        assemblies nobody had added. Fifty-three of them are rooted here now and the remaining
        thirteen are in NotRooted.txt with what an ILC publish actually said about each.

        ⚠ **The expensive half of #506 was its prediction, not the work.** That issue expected
        "Vixen.Ui's reactive graph and Vixen.Core.Serialization's consumers" to be the costly ones,
        and said each newly rooted assembly is "a fresh set of IL2xxx/IL3xxx findings to fix, and
        `ILLinkTreatWarningsAsErrors` means every one is a build break until it is". Measured by
        rooting all sixty-six at once: **twelve findings in the whole set**, six of them in one
        developer-loop assembly and six of them inside Silk.NET rather than in Vixen. Vixen.Rendering
        and all ten of its siblings, Vixen.Net and all nine of its, Vixen.Animation, Vixen.Ai,
        Vixen.Graphics.OpenGL, the three desktop platform heads and — the one this repository
        expected to cost most — Vixen.Core.Yaml, a serializer, were every one of them clean on the
        first publish.

        ⚠ Every entry here needs a matching TrimmerRootAssembly below or it is covered only by what
        Main happens to reach, which proves nothing about the rest of it. `nuke CheckAot` fails on
        that mismatch rather than trusting an author to notice.
    -->
